::GEO_U01_L07_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which reason supports the conclusion &angle;1 ≅ &angle;2 when &angle;1 and &angle;2 are vertical angles? {
=Vertical angles are congruent #Correct. This known angle relationship supports the conclusion.
~The angles look equal #Appearance alone is not a valid proof reason.
~The angles have the same label #Labels do not prove congruence.
~The angles are drawn near each other #Location in a diagram does not prove congruence.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>If &angle;A and &angle;B form a linear pair, which statement is supported? {
~m&angle;A = m&angle;B #A linear pair does not always create equal angles.
=m&angle;A + m&angle;B = 180° #Correct. Angles that form a linear pair are supplementary.
~m&angle;A + m&angle;B = 90° #A linear pair forms a straight angle, not necessarily a right angle.
~&angle;A and &angle;B are vertical angles #A linear pair is adjacent, not vertical.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which counterexample disproves the claim All quadrilaterals are rectangles? {
~A square #A square is a rectangle, so it does not disprove the claim.
~A rectangle with side lengths 3 and 5 #This is still a rectangle, so it does not disprove the claim.
=A rhombus with no right angles #Correct. This is a quadrilateral that is not a rectangle.
~A triangle #A triangle is not a quadrilateral, so it cannot disprove a claim about quadrilaterals.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>What is the first step in a proof by contradiction? {
~Draw a diagram that looks correct #A diagram may help, but contradiction proof begins with an assumption.
=Assume the opposite of what you want to prove #Correct. A contradiction proof starts by assuming the negation of the desired conclusion.
~State the conclusion without a reason #A proof must justify each conclusion.
~Find one example that works #One example does not establish a proof by contradiction.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>Why is the argument The lines are parallel because they look parallel invalid? {
~Parallel lines cannot appear in diagrams #Parallel lines can appear in diagrams, but appearance alone is not proof.
~All diagrams are always drawn to scale #Diagrams are not always drawn to scale.
~Parallel lines never need evidence #Parallel conclusions require valid evidence.
=It relies on appearance instead of a valid reason #Correct. A valid argument must connect the conclusion to accepted evidence.
}
